---
import Table from "../Table.astro";
import Forms from "./Forms.astro";
const headers = ["Person", "Singular", "Dual", "Plural"];
const persons: [string, string, string] = ["3rd (Prathama)", "2nd (Madhyama)", "1st (Uttama)"];
---

<h2>1. Dhātu: The Root of Every Verb</h2>
<p>
  Every Sanskrit verb is built from a <strong>dhātu (धातु)</strong>, a verbal root that carries the basic meaning: <code>पठ्</code> (read), <code>गम्</code> (go), <code>कृ</code> (do). Pāṇini's list of roots, the <em>Dhātupāṭha</em>, contains around two thousand of them, arranged in ten classes (<em>gaṇas</em>) according to how they form their present stem.
</p>
<p>
  To a root we add endings called <strong>tiṅ</strong> that show person, number and voice. The set of endings depends on the tense or mood, which Sanskrit grammar calls a <strong>lakāra (लकार)</strong>, because Pāṇini named each one with a technical term beginning with <code>ल्</code>.
</p>

<h2>2. The Ten Lakāras</h2>
<Table
  headers={["Lakāra", "Meaning", "Example (पठ्)"]}
  rows={[
    ["लट् (laṭ)", "Present", "पठति – reads"],
    ["लिट् (liṭ)", "Perfect (remote past)", "पपाठ – read (long ago)"],
    ["लुट् (luṭ)", "Periphrastic future", "पठिता – will read"],
    ["लृट् (lṛṭ)", "Simple future", "पठिष्यति – will read"],
    ["लेट् (leṭ)", "Subjunctive (Vedic only)", "—"],
    ["लोट् (loṭ)", "Imperative", "पठतु – let him read"],
    ["लङ् (laṅ)", "Imperfect (past)", "अपठत् – read"],
    ["लिङ् (liṅ)", "Optative / benedictive", "पठेत् – should read"],
    ["लुङ् (luṅ)", "Aorist", "अपाठीत् – read"],
    ["लृङ् (lṛṅ)", "Conditional", "अपठिष्यत् – would have read"],
  ]}
/>
<p>
  School syllabuses (CBSE Classes 6–10) concentrate on five: <strong>लट्, लृट्, लङ्, लोट्</strong> and <strong>विधिलिङ्</strong>. Master these and you can read most simple prose.
</p>

<h2>3. Person, Number and Voice</h2>
<p>
  Each lakāra has <strong>nine forms</strong>: three persons × three numbers. Note that Sanskrit grammar counts persons in the opposite order to English:
</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><strong>Prathama puruṣa</strong> (3rd person): he, she, it, they — and every noun subject.</li>
  <li><strong>Madhyama puruṣa</strong> (2nd person): used only with <code>युष्मद्</code> (त्वम्, युवाम्, यूयम्).</li>
  <li><strong>Uttama puruṣa</strong> (1st person): used only with <code>अस्मद्</code> (अहम्, आवाम्, वयम्).</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Roots also take one of two sets of endings: <strong>Parasmaipada</strong> (e.g. <code>पठति</code>) or <strong>Ātmanepada</strong> (e.g. <code>लभते</code>). Some roots, called <em>ubhayapada</em>, can take either (<code>करोति</code> / <code>कुरुते</code>).
</p>

<h2>4. Conjugation Tables for पठ् (Parasmaipada)</h2>
<h3>लट् – Present tense</h3>
<Forms lakara="lat" headers={headers} persons={persons} />
<h3>लृट् – Future tense</h3>
<Forms lakara="lrt" headers={headers} persons={persons} />
<h3>लङ् – Past tense</h3>
<p>The past tense adds the augment <code>अ-</code> before the root.</p>
<Forms lakara="lan" headers={headers} persons={persons} />
<h3>लोट् – Imperative (commands and requests)</h3>
<Forms lakara="lot" headers={headers} persons={persons} />
<h3>विधिलिङ् – Optative ("should")</h3>
<Forms lakara="vidhilin" headers={headers} persons={persons} />

<h2>5. An Ātmanepada Example: लभ् (to obtain), लट्</h2>
<Forms lakara="labh-lat" headers={headers} persons={persons} />

<h2>6. Making the Verb Agree</h2>
<p>
  The verb must match its subject in person and number. Gender does not matter for finite verbs.
</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><code>बालकः पठति।</code> – The boy reads. (3rd, singular)</li>
  <li><code>बालिकाः पठन्ति।</code> – The girls read. (3rd, plural)</li>
  <li><code>त्वं श्वः पठिष्यसि।</code> – You will read tomorrow. (2nd, singular, लृट्)</li>
  <li><code>वयं ह्यः अपठाम।</code> – We read yesterday. (1st, plural, लङ्)</li>
</ul>
<p>
  A quick check for translation exercises: first find the subject, decide its person and number, then pick the lakāra from the time word (<code>अद्य</code> today, <code>ह्यः</code> yesterday, <code>श्वः</code> tomorrow) and choose the matching ending from the tables above.
</p>
